<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhengdi Yu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Simone Foti
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Linguang Zhang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Meta Reality Labs
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Amy Zhao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Meta Reality Labs
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Cem Keskin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Meta Reality Labs
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Stefanos Zafeiriou
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tolga Birdal
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Imperial College London
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce Neural Riemannian Motion Fields (NRMF), a novel 3D generative human motion prior that enables robust, temporally consistent, and physically plausible 3D motion recovery. Unlike existing VAE or diffusion-based methods, our higher-order motion prior explicitly models the human motion in the zero level set of a collection of neural distance fields (NDFs) corresponding to pose, transition (velocity), and acceleration dynamics. Our framework is rigorous in the sense that our NDFs are constructed on the product space of joint rotations, their angular velocities, and angular accelerations, respecting the geometry of the underlying articulations. We further introduce: (i) a novel adaptive-step hybrid algorithm for projecting onto the set of plausible motions, and (ii) a novel geometric integrator to “roll out” realistic motion trajectories during test-time-optimization and generation.
Our experiments show significant and consistent gains: trained on the AMASS dataset, NRMF remarkably generalizes across multiple input modalities and to diverse tasks ranging from denoising to motion in-betweening and fitting to partial 2D / 3D observations. Our project page is available <a href="https://circle-group.github.io/research/NRMF/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_bold">here</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block">
    
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para"><img src="2509.09667v1/teaser3.png" id="g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/165;" width="685" height="165" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</div>
    
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S0.F1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NRMF</span> is a general-purpose, expressive and robust unconditional motion prior. It models the space of plausible <span id="S0.F1.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">poses</span> (<math id="S0.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>), <span id="S0.F1.9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">transitions</span> (<math id="S0.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <span id="S0.F1.9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">accelerations</span> (<math id="S0.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">¨</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>) on the zero-level set of a <span id="S0.F1.9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">geometric neural distance field</span>. This implicitly captures the data distribution.
Poses are depicted alongside their transitions and accelerations, which are visualized as blue dots onto the per-joint distributions of learned transitions and as blue rings around the magnitude distribution of all accelerations.
We develop projection (<math id="S0.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>) and integration algorithms to deploy NRMF into several applications as shown on the right. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motion is intrinsic to human experience. As humans, we are in perpetual motion, engaging with our surroundings in rich and diverse ways. Accurately recovering 3D human motion from sparse or noisy data is therefore not only a fundamental challenge in computer vision, but also a critical enabler for a myriad of applications, from immersive virtual reality and animation to clinical diagnostics, and sports analysis. Despite remarkable progress in learning-based techniques and model-based reconstructions, current methods still struggle to deliver reliable, temporally consistent, and physically plausible 3D motion recovery while being able to generate diverse and <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lifelike</em> motions.
The challenges stem from the intrinsic complexity of human motion, compounded by occlusions, rapid movements, and inherent ambiguities in 2D observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we focus on the problem of building <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">general-purpose</em>, <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expressive</em> and <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robust</em> models that capture the full dynamics of <em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">realistic</em> human motion.
A conventional way to handle sequences of articulations is to model static poses, frame-by-frame as done in NRDF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, PoseNDF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> or DPoser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. Yet, processing frames independently jeopardizes generalization and temporal consistency.
As a remedy, modern models of motion have shifted toward dynamics-based methods, many of which rely either on
VAEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> suffering from <em id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">posterior-collapse</em> and increased accumulation of errors over time (<em id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">drift</em>) or leverage the recent diffusion modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, which while promising, tend to capture short pose sequences in clean settings or require additional conditioning such as action or textual descriptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Diffusion-based models further suffer from increased inference times and their conditioning to observations (<em id="S1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inversion</em>) is far from being trivial. While RoHM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> tries to bridge this gap, as we will demonstrate, it suffers from low estimation accuracy, due to the limited handling of higher-order motion dynamics, such as acceleration, causing oversmoothed transitions and drift. MoManifold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> factors in the accelerations. Yet, beyond being limited to short, fixed time windows, its <em id="S1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decoupled manifold</em> treats each joint’s acceleration in isolation, neglecting the essential inter-dependencies of the body joints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Neural Riemannian Motion Fields</span> (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NRMF</span>), a novel human motion prior that models the manifold of plausible motions as a collection of three neural distance fields (NDFs), each corresponding to 0th-order (pose), 1st-order (pose transitions), and crucially, 2nd-order (acceleration) dynamics, captured as the zero level sets of dedicated conditioned, implicit neural networks (<a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Modeling of Plausible Articulated Motions ‣ 3 Neural Riemannian Motion Fields ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>b).
We rigorously respect the underlying geometry of articulated motion by operating in the product space of joint rotations, angular velocities, and angular accelerations.
To map an arbitrary articulated motion onto a plausible one (<a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Modeling of Plausible Articulated Motions ‣ 3 Neural Riemannian Motion Fields ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>a), we introduce a three-stage adaptive-step hybrid gradient descent. We further devise a novel <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">geometric</em> and <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robust integrator</em> to <em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rollout</em> physically realistic motion sequences, while correcting errors in all dynamic components.
Together, these enable a plethora of applications as exemplified in <a href="#S0.F1" title="In Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After training on the large AMASS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> motion capture dataset, we extensively evaluate NRMF on numerous tasks including motion estimation, denoising, in-betweening, and fitting to various input modalities, over various datasets including AMASS, i3DB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, 3DPW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, EgoBody <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> and PROX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Our quantitative results show <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">significant and consistent improvements</span> across several standard metrics for assessing human motion. Notably, a qualitative investigation into the learned pose, transition and acceleration distributions reveals the expressivity of our method.
To summarize, our contributions are:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose to model poses, transitions and accelerations as the zero level sets of corresponding product NDFs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce new algorithms for projecting onto the zero level sets as well as for rolling out physically plausible motion sequences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We ensure that all our algorithms respect the geometry of articulated rotations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From sampling motions to fitting 2D/3D observations, we demonstrate that NRMF surpasses all prominent state-of-the-art methods across several tasks and metrics.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We additionally present a condensed primer on the geometry of articulated motions in our suppl. material as a self-contained background. We will make our implementation publicly available upon publication.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unconditional human pose and motion priors</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human bodies have been modeled by unconditional priors of various different kinds including Gaussian Processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, VAEs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, NDFs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
Yet, per-frame inference is not sufficient to capture the full complexity of motion. To this end, various works such as MotionVAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, HuMoR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> and
PhaseMP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> leveraged VAEs in an autoregressive fashion. With the proliferation of diffusion models and transformers, modern approaches such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> leverage transformer-based diffusion frameworks to generate diverse motion. Unlike these works, we take a fundamentally different approach and offer a strong generative prior that is deployable for recovering human motion from observations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Monocular human pose and motion recovery</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Approaches for estimating human pose and shape from monocular observations can be categorized into regression-based and optimization-based. Regression-based methods predict pose directly from images or videos using deep networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> which are typically based on the parameteric models of SMPL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, SMPL-H <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> or SMPL-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, but often lack temporal consistency, and fail under occlusion. Optimization-based approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> fit parametric models to observations while enforcing temporal priors, achieving better robustness but at the cost of high computation, and sensitivity to initialization. Recent methods also integrate learned motion priors via diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, improving plausibility and infilling. RoHM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> reconstructs motion via diffusion denoising of root and local pose spaces with image and physics guidance. However, it still suffers from oversmoothing and cannot fully exploit higher-order dynamics. Our method NRMF explicitly models pose, velocity, and acceleration via neural distance fields on Riemannian motion manifolds, enabling physically plausible recovery across diverse inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Neural Riemannian Motion Fields</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by explaining <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Riemannian Motion Fields</span> to model realistic articulated motions in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Modeling of Plausible Articulated Motions ‣ 3 Neural Riemannian Motion Fields ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and introduce our novel projection algorithm to map onto this space while adhering to the manifold of joint rotations, transitions and accelerations. We then propose NRMF and a novel method for sampling articulated motions, to generate desired training data, in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Learning Riemannian Motion Fields (NRMF) ‣ 3 Neural Riemannian Motion Fields ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Before delving into method specifics, we briefly review the <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-kinematics and provide a detailed, self-contained exposition in our suppl. material.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Background</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are particularly interested in the non-commutative Lie group of finite rigid body rotations:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdfn1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmdfn1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmdfn1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set of rotation matrices admit a group structure under matrix multiplication:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}:\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}=\mathbf{I},\operatorname{det}(\mathbf{R})=1\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>det</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}:\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}=\mathbf{I},\operatorname{det}(\mathbf{R})=1\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now briefly characterize the properties of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmprop1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tangent space of <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined throughout the paper as the <em id="Thmprop1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">left-invariant vector fields</em>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\,:\,\bm{\Omega}\in\mathfrak{so}(3)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\,:\,\bm{\Omega}\in\mathfrak{so}(3)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{so}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{so}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the set of <em id="Thmprop1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">skew-symmetric</em> tensors:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{so}(3)=\left\{\bm{\Omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}:\bm{\Omega}^{\top}=-\bm{\Omega}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{so}(3)=\left\{\bm{\Omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}:\bm{\Omega}^{\top}=-\bm{\Omega}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Differentiating the constraints that specify <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> leads us to the definition of skew-symmetric matrices:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R})}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}=\dot{\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\mathbf{R}+\mathbf{R}^{\top}\dot{\mathbf{R}}=0\implies\bm{\Omega}=-\bm{\Omega}^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R})}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}=\dot{\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\mathbf{R}+\mathbf{R}^{\top}\dot{\mathbf{R}}=0\implies\bm{\Omega}=-\bm{\Omega}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Omega}=\mathbf{R}^{\top}\dot{\mathbf{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Omega}=\mathbf{R}^{\top}\dot{\mathbf{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a skew symmetric matrix and the vector <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{R}}=\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{R}}=\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We also need the notions of angular velocity and acceleration on <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> to accurately model articulated motions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdfn2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Angular velocity)<span id="Thmdfn2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <em id="Thmdfn2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">angular velocity</em> of the geodesic motion on <math id="Thmdfn2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> is represented as a time (<math id="Thmdfn2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>) dependent curve <math id="Thmdfn2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\omega}}_{t}:=\omega(t):=\skewrm\,({{\Omega}}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>skew</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\omega}}_{t}:=\omega(t):=\skewrm\,({{\Omega}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}=\mathbf{R}_{t}^{-1}\dot{\mathbf{R}}_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}=\mathbf{R}_{t}^{-1}\dot{\mathbf{R}}_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdfn2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\skewrm(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.059em;" width="2.059em"><mi>skew</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\skewrm(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> transforms the matrix into a <em id="Thmdfn2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">skew-vector</em> and
the <span id="Thmdfn2.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">angular velocity matrix</span> <math id="Thmdfn2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}:=\Omega(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}:=\Omega(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is skew-symmetric.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmprop2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Angular acceleration)<span id="Thmprop2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The time-derivative of angular velocity defines the angular acceleration:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}[{\bm{\omega}}_{t}]_{x}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}:=[\dot{\bm{\omega}}_{t}]_{x}:=\ddot{\mathbf{R}}_{t}=\mathbf{R}_{t}\left({\bm{\Omega}}_{t}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}[{\bm{\omega}}_{t}]_{x}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}:=[\dot{\bm{\omega}}_{t}]_{x}:=\ddot{\mathbf{R}}_{t}=\mathbf{R}_{t}\left({\bm{\Omega}}_{t}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmprop2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also skew-symmetric and <math id="Thmprop2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{R}}_{t}:={\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\mathbf{R}_{t}/}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{R}}_{t}:={\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}\mathbf{R}_{t}/}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we use a central differencing scheme to estimate the angular velocity and acceleration. As we show in our suppl. material, this is a good approximation of the true acceleration computed via <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math>-maps and <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel transport</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our work differs significantly from modeling motion in pure <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> also because of the specific <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">product manifold</em> structure which we discuss next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Modeling of Plausible Articulated Motions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider an input motion sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> composed of individual states <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdfn3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(State)<span id="Thmdfn3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We represent the state of a moving articulated body as a time dependent function <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t):t\to\mathbf{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t):t\to\mathbf{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> producing the matrix <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{t}=x(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{t}=x(t)</annotation></semantics></math> composed of a root translation <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, body joint angles <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}\in\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}\in\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}}</annotation></semantics></math>, their velocities <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}\in\mathcal{T}\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}\in\mathcal{T}\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}}</annotation></semantics></math> and angular accelerations <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\bm{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times{N_{J}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\bm{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times{N_{J}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{X}=[\;\;\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}}\;\;\bm{\theta}\;\;\dot{\bm{\theta}}\;\;\ddot{\bm{\theta}}\;\;]\;\in\;(\mathbb{R}^{3}\times\mathcal{M})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.560em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{X}=[\;\;\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}}\;\;\bm{\theta}\;\;\dot{\bm{\theta}}\;\;\ddot{\bm{\theta}}\;\;]\;\in\;(\mathbb{R}^{3}\times\mathcal{M}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{J}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the total number of joints excluding the hand joints (<math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{J}=22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{J}=22</annotation></semantics></math> for SMPL model) and <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the product space of the state vector including the root orientation: <math id="Thmdfn3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\left(\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}}\times\mathfrak{so}(3)^{N_{J}}\times\mathbb{R}^{3\times N_{J}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔰</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\left(\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}}\times\mathfrak{so}(3)^{N_{J}}\times\mathbb{R}^{3\times N_{J}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first part of the state involving root translation is known as the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">global motion</span>, whereas the rest parameterize the <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">local motion</span>, the motion of the skeleton.
Part of the state, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterizes the articulated object model (e.g., SMPL for humans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> or MANO for hands <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>) which is a differentiable function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math> mapping joints <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{J}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{J}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times J}</annotation></semantics></math> and shape parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\beta}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\beta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\beta}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\beta}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the vertices <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times N_{\bm{V}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝑽</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times N_{\bm{V}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of an articulated object, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\bm{V}}=6890" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝑽</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6890</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\bm{V}}=6890</annotation></semantics></math> for SMPL human bodies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of parameterizing the entire motion sequence as done in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, we propose to model the current state (pose, transition and acceleration) while respecting the geometry:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmprop3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Riemannian Motion Fields (RMFs))<span id="Thmprop3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the state, we model the manifold of instantaneous <span id="Thmprop3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">realistic and plausible motions</span> of an articulated body as the zero level set of an implicit function <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}:\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\{\mathbf{X}^{\mathcal{M}}\in\mathcal{M}\,\mid\,f_{\Gamma}(\mathbf{X}^{\mathcal{M}})=\mathbf{0}\}\textrm{,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\{\mathbf{X}^{\mathcal{M}}\in\mathcal{M}\,\mid\,f_{\Gamma}(\mathbf{X}^{\mathcal{M}})=\mathbf{0}\}\textrm{,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that the range of <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> (parameterized by <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>) represents the unsigned geodesic distance to the closest plausible pose on the manifold. To account for the interdependence of pose, velocity and acceleration we disentangle <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> into conditional components as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}=\left[\begin{array}[]{c}f_{\Phi}^{R}({\bm{\theta}})\\
f_{\Psi}^{\omega}({\dot{\bm{\theta}}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}})\\
f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}({\ddot{\bm{\theta}}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta},\dot{\bm{\theta}}})\\
\end{array}\right]\begin{array}[]{l}\emph{(pose~field)}\\
\emph{(transition~field)}\\
\emph{(acceleration~field)}\\
\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">∣</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}=\left[\begin{array}[]{c}f_{\Phi}^{R}({\bm{\theta}})\\
f_{\Psi}^{\omega}({\dot{\bm{\theta}}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}})\\
f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}({\ddot{\bm{\theta}}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta},\dot{\bm{\theta}}})\\
\end{array}\right]\begin{array}[]{l}\emph{(pose~field)}\\
\emph{(transition~field)}\\
\emph{(acceleration~field)}\\
\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(RMF)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Phi}^{R}:\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}}\to\mathbb{R}_{+},f_{\Psi}^{\omega}:\mathfrak{so}(3)^{N_{J}}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Phi}^{R}:\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}}\to\mathbb{R}_{+},f_{\Psi}^{\omega}:\mathfrak{so}(3)^{N_{J}}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}:\mathbb{R}^{3\times N_{J}}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}:\mathbb{R}^{3\times N_{J}}\to\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> account for the plausibility in pose, velocity and acceleration, respectively. <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=[\Phi,\Psi,\Xi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=[\Phi,\Psi,\Xi]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/NRMF_singlecol.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/273;" width="685" height="273" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Neural Riemannian Motion Fields (<span id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NRMF</span>) models the motion in the zero-level-set of three disjoint distance fields.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to state our iterations for projecting onto this zero level set specified by <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmprop4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RMF-Grad)<span id="Thmprop4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A geometry-aware single step projection of a given state onto our motion manifold involves the updates:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{t+1}:=\Pi^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{t+1}:=\Pi^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Exp}_{\bm{\theta}_{t}}\left(-\alpha_{\theta}f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})\,\frac{\mathrm{grad}f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})}{\|\mathrm{grad}f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})\|}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Exp</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>grad</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>grad</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Exp}_{\bm{\theta}_{t}}\left(-\alpha_{\theta}f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})\,\frac{\mathrm{grad}f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})}{\|\mathrm{grad}f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})\|}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t+1}:=\Pi^{\omega}_{t}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t+1}:=\Pi^{\omega}_{t}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}-\alpha_{\dot{\theta}}f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})\,\frac{\nabla{f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}_{t})}}{\|\nabla{f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}_{t})}\|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}-\alpha_{\dot{\theta}}f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})\,\frac{\nabla{f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}_{t})}}{\|\nabla{f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}_{t})}\|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t+1}:=\Pi^{\dot{\omega}}_{t}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>t</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t+1}:=\Pi^{\dot{\omega}}_{t}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}-\alpha_{\dot{\omega}}f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})\,\frac{\nabla{f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}_{t},\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})}}{\|\nabla{f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}_{t},\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})}\|}\textnormal{,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.170em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}-\alpha_{\dot{\omega}}f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})\,\frac{\nabla{f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}_{t},\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})}}{\|\nabla{f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\,\mid\,\bm{\theta}_{t},\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})}\|}\textnormal{,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to individual learning rates and <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{grad}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>grad</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{grad}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmprop4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{{Exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{{Exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <span id="Thmprop4.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Riemannian gradient</span> and <span id="Thmprop4.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exponential maps</span> as we provide in our suppl. material.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note that under ideal conditions where transition (velocities) are obtained computationally, we expect:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{\omega}\left(\dot{\bm{\theta}}\right)\approx\left(\Pi^{R}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)^{\top}\dot{\Pi^{R}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{\omega}\left(\dot{\bm{\theta}}\right)\approx\left(\Pi^{R}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)^{\top}\dot{\Pi^{R}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and leverage the right hand side, accounting for the use of noisy input velocities. Likewise, <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}})\approx{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}(\Pi^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}))}/{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}})\approx{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}(\Pi^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}))}/{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A geometry-aware robust integrator</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now provide a novel <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deterministic</em> integration algorithm for <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rolling out</span> motion sequences starting from a plausible initial state. Equivalently, it will allow us to <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rebuild</em> a consistent state sequence from the projected measurements, a key step in motion generation and test-time-optimization as we will explain later.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5</span></span><span id="Thmprop5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RMF-Integrator)<span id="Thmprop5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a moving articulated body <math id="Thmprop5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(t):t\to\mathbf{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(t):t\to\mathbf{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the “ideal” continuous dynamics are governed by the differential equations in <a href="#S1.E35" title="In Definition 9 (Angular velocity). ‣ Riemannian manifolds ‣ A Geometry of Articulated Motions ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E6" title="Eq. 6 ‣ Proposition 2 (Angular acceleration). ‣ Background ‣ 3 Neural Riemannian Motion Fields ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. We then apply a geometric, projected <span id="Thmprop5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Euler integration</span> to those factoring in our priors. Given a sequence of plausible accelerations, this leads to the following discrete updates:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Pi^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}+\lambda_{t}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Pi^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}+\lambda_{t}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\theta}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Pi^{R}\left(\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\bm{\theta}_{t}}\bigl(\alpha_{t}\,[{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}_{t}]_{x}\bigr)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Exp</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Pi^{R}\left(\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\bm{\theta}_{t}}\bigl(\alpha_{t}\,[{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}_{t}]_{x}\bigr)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In practice, we first update the velocities before integrating for the rotations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The pseudocode for our integrator is provided in our suppl. material.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Relation to known operators and integrators)<span id="Thmremark2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> corresponds to NRDF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> while the other two projectors resemble Euclidean PoseNDF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> updates but for velocities and accelerations. Beyond ensuring plausibility of motion, our RMF-Integrator can be thought of as a <span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">denoising</span> of the trajectory and its derivatives or a <span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">drift correction</span> that helps mitigate the accumulation of errors.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Learning Riemannian Motion Fields (NRMF)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe how we construct <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, learn all components of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> indicating distances to the zero level set.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6</span></span><span id="Thmprop6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(NRMF)<span id="Thmprop6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.1 Modeling of Plausible Articulated Motions ‣ 3 Neural Riemannian Motion Fields ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we model <math id="Thmprop6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> hierarchically by three individual neural fields corresponding to different orders of derivatives in the equation of motion, trained to predict the distance to the closest example from dataset <math id="Thmprop6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=[\mathcal{D}_{\theta},\dot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta},\ddot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=[\mathcal{D}_{\theta},\dot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta},\ddot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}]</annotation></semantics></math> as follows<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Note, for brevity, we dropped the conditionals.</span></span></span></span>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.Ex4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:173.5pt;vertical-align:-80.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(144.2pt,-50.4pt) scale(2.38422760497527,2.38422760497527) ;">
<p id="S3.Ex4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Gamma^{\star}}=\left[\begin{aligned} {\Phi^{\star}}&amp;=\argmin_{\Phi}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N}\lVert f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{i})-\min_{\bm{\theta}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}_{\theta}}d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}^{N_{J}}(\bm{\theta}_{i},\bm{\theta}^{\prime})\rVert\\
{\Psi^{\star}}&amp;=\argmin_{\Psi}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N}\lVert f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})-\min_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}^{\prime}\in\dot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}}d_{\mathfrak{so}(3)}^{N_{J}}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i},\dot{\bm{\theta}}^{\prime})\rVert\\
{\Xi^{\star}}&amp;=\argmin_{\Xi}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N}\lVert f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})-\min_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}^{\prime}\in\ddot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}}d_{\mathbb{R}^{3}}^{N_{J}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i},\ddot{\bm{\theta}}^{\prime})\rVert\end{aligned}\right]\textnormal{.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>R</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Gamma^{\star}}=\left[\begin{aligned} {\Phi^{\star}}&amp;=\argmin_{\Phi}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N}\lVert f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{i})-\min_{\bm{\theta}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}_{\theta}}d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}^{N_{J}}(\bm{\theta}_{i},\bm{\theta}^{\prime})\rVert\\
{\Psi^{\star}}&amp;=\argmin_{\Psi}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N}\lVert f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})-\min_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}^{\prime}\in\dot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}}d_{\mathfrak{so}(3)}^{N_{J}}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i},\dot{\bm{\theta}}^{\prime})\rVert\\
{\Xi^{\star}}&amp;=\argmin_{\Xi}\sum\limits_{i=1}^{N}\lVert f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})-\min_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}^{\prime}\in\ddot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}}d_{\mathbb{R}^{3}}^{N_{J}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i},\ddot{\bm{\theta}}^{\prime})\rVert\end{aligned}\right]\textnormal{.}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We call <math id="Thmprop6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, learned in this way, a <em id="Thmprop6.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">neural Riemannian motion field</em>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each individual function is modeled by a combination of a hierarchical network and an MLP decoder, similar to NRDF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and Pose-NDF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>.
We obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{grad}f_{\Gamma}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>grad</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{grad}f_{\Gamma}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> via backprop, additionally followed by <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">egrad2rgrad</em> for the poses themselves.
Given a dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\theta}=\{\bm{\theta}_{i}\}_{1\leq i\leq N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\theta}=\{\bm{\theta}_{i}\}_{1\leq i\leq N}</annotation></semantics></math> of articulated poses, we obtain the dataset of transitions (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>) and accelerations (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathcal{D}}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>) by computing the angular velocities and accelerations as explained earlier.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The first part of this equation corresponds to the recent NRDF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, which builds upon PoseNDF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> and correctly treats the Riemannian geometry of articulated 3D poses using quaternions. The acceleration field in the last part vaguely resembles MoManifold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, while properly using the joints orientations instead of 3D positions.
The <span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">transition prior</span> in the middle row bears similarities with various works which model transitions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, inspired mostly by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. A majority of these works use some form of autoencoding, which is fundamentally different from our approach.
To the best of our knowledge, we are the firsts to properly model all components of the kinematics, while respecting the geometry of parameters.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>NRMF as Versatile Generative Priors</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce multiple downstream applications. Utilizing our NRFM as a generative prior, we can enable: 1) test-time optimization, 2) motion generation and 3) motion in-betweening, as shown in <a href="#S0.F1" title="In Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Test-time optimization</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a novel runtime optimization objective to recover a plausible (<em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">likely</em> under the NRMF) motion sequence <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on a given sequence of 2D or 3D data observations, <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span> joint detections, point clouds, described as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{O}}_{0:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐎</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{O}}_{0:T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our mutlti-stage optimization starts from a proper initialization using the pose prior, by minimizing a HuMoR-like objective in stage I:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{I}(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}+\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}+\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}+\lambda_{reg}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{I}(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}+\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}+\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}+\lambda_{reg}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}=\|\bm{\beta}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}=\|\bm{\beta}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the shape prior<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            We will later optimize for the shape parameters (<math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) per sequence.</span></span></span>. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}=f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}=f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is our pose prior term predicting the distance to the nearest plausible pose<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Note that for VPoser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}=\|\mathbf{z}\|^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}=\|\mathbf{z}\|^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is the latent code.</span></span></span>. We further employ additional regularization terms <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math> to constrain the bone lengths and smoothness of the skeleton as described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.
Finally, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the task-dependent data terms and will be different when given different types of inputs: 2D, 3D joints or 3D point clouds. We provide complete details of these in our supplementary material.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/all_distribs2.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/156;" width="685" height="156" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of pose and transition distributions.<span id="S4.F3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Each distribution is estimated from the points obtained intersecting rotated skeletal bones with a sphere centered at the joint. The pose distributions <math id="S4.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained applying <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> rotations (<span id="S4.F3.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">left</span>), while the transition distributions <math id="S4.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\dot{\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\dot{\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> applying <math id="S4.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S4.F3.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">right</span>). Each sphere effectively displays the range of motion of eah joint. </span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While optimizing <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> yields refined estimates <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, it cannot handle motion ambiguities since the pose prior only captures <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0^{th}</annotation></semantics></math>-order distribution. After initialization, we factor in our transition and acceleration priors as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}:=f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})\quad\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}:=f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}:=f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})\quad\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}:=f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By further combining foot-floor contact physics constraint terms into <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math> in stage II, the full objective function reads:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{II}^{\prime}(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{II}^{\prime}(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}+\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}+\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}+\lambda_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}+\lambda_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}+\lambda_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}+\lambda_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we apply the rollout within each iteration to further increase precision. We provide more details in the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motion generation &amp; in-betweening</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our motion prior can be utilized to generate plausible and diverse motions. To this end, inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we start from a random initialization of all motion components. In particular, given an initial motion state <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we randomly sample a noisy transition <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and generate the next <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> using the transition. By repeating this procedure in a rollout manner, we obtain a noisy partial motion sequence with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{\theta}_{t},\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\}^{T}_{t=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{\theta}_{t},\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\}^{T}_{t=0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The noisy acceleration <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then computed based on it to compose the complete initial noisy motion sequences <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tilde{\mathbf{X}_{t}}\}^{T}_{t=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tilde{\mathbf{X}_{t}}\}^{T}_{t=0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we run our RMF-Integrator taken the sequence as input to denoise and obtain the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}^{T}_{t=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}^{T}_{t=0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is subsequently treated as the initial noisy observation for test-time optimization (Sec. <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">4</span>) similar to <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MoManifold</span> to obtain the final clean motion from the randomly composed disordered starting point. For in-betweening, given a sequence of incomplete observations, we preprocess the unobserved poses with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> and then apply the optimization process with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> nd <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{I}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{I}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> treating the observed frames as noisy data observation inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. Unlike <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, our method is not restricted to clean input motions, it can be applied to partial masked body infilling, and it also deduces natural transitions between masked between-frame with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, while respecting the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{\text{nd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mtext>nd</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{\text{nd}}</annotation></semantics></math>-order dynamics via the acceleration prior <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now assess the performance of our method across various downstream tasks: (i) motion estimation from 3D observations, (ii) motion refinement for human mesh recovery, (iii) motion estimation from RGB(-D) in-the-wild (2D) observations and (iv) motion generation and in-betweening. Additionally, we conduct ablation studies to demonstrate the contribution of each prior. We recommend viewing the <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;">supplementary videos</span> for qualitative results illustrating our approach. Further details of the dataset and experimental setup are provided in our suppl. material.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we leverage the following datasets:</p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AMASS</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> is a large-scale human motion database comprising of high-quality and diverse motion captures. For training and evaluation, we subsample the dataset at a rate of 30 Hz and follow the split used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. We extract transitions (velocities) and corresponding accelerations by sampling 80% of each motion sequence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3DPW</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> is a popular benchmark for 3D human pose and shape estimation in challenging, real-world settings. Provides high-quality 3D pose annotations captured through synchronized IMUs and motion capture systems, along with RGB videos recorded in diverse outdoor environments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">i3DB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> is an RGB dataset of human-scene interactions, providing 3D joint annotations along with 3D scene reconstructions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EgoBody</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> is a large-scale dataset of 3D human motion captures during social interactions in 3D environments, with one subject wearing a head-mounted device recording egocentric multi-modal data, including eye gaze tracking, synchronized multi-view RGBD video, and full-body 3D motion reconstruction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PROX</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> is an RGB-D dataset that provides 3D human pose and shape data in 3D real-world indoor environments with interactions between humans and surroundings. We pre-process them to obtain the observations in the same way as i3DB.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
