<td id="S5.T2.5.1.21.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.5.1.21.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-bg-color:#ECECEC;">3.05%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.5.1.21.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.5.1.21.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-bg-color:#ECECEC;">1.9</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.5.1.21.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.5.1.21.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-bg-color:#ECECEC;">2.31</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To perform motion denoising, we align the recovered human body well with the observations and preserve the plausibility of the motion. To test this, we sample <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#5E7B8B;">noisy keypoints</span> from the AMASS dataset with a Gaussian noise of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4cm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4cm</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in <a href="#S5.T1" title="In Datasets ‣ 5.1 Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we compare NRMF with state-of-the-art pose <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and motion priors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. It can be seen that our method consistently outperforms the SotA. Previous methods typically only focus on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-order or <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1^{\text{st}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>1</mn><mtext>st</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1^{\text{st}}</annotation></semantics></math>-order cues either neglecting the high-order information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> thus producing unrealistic, oversmooth, and implausible motion transitions, or causing error accumulation over time (drift) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. In contrast, our method incorporates priors for all essential components of dynamics to fill in the plausibility gap while maintaining the fidelity of each frame, which is supported by the comparison of the output distribution in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Test-time optimization ‣ 4 NRMF as Versatile Generative Priors ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Moreover, as shown in <a href="#S5.T1" title="In Datasets ‣ 5.1 Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, methods that properly treat the geometry of articulations tend to perform better, while PoseNDF based (Euclidean) projections fail under larger noise.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure" style="width:49%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09667v1/final_explicit_arial_square.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:343/339;" width="343" height="339"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Behavior of different methods for increasing levels of noise.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">MDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> and RoHM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> present comparable results for contact plausibility but yield low estimation accuracy. <a href="#S5.F4" title="In Motion estimation from 3D observations ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> demonstrates the effect of gradual (Gaussian) noising on MPVPE, where our method consistently performs better and behaves more stable under increased noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To test motion estimation under occlusions (<em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#5E7B8B;">partial observation</span>) we sample the AMASS dataset with a standard mask height of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9m</annotation></semantics></math> following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> to yield partial 3D observations.
<a href="#S5.T2" title="In Motion estimation from 3D observations ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reveals that while applying only a pose prior term (<em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text"></span> <em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">-t</em>)
gives lower errors for visible body parts, the model suffers from over-smoothing and higher reconstruction and acceleration errors when deducing invisible body parts over time, due to the inherent negligence of transitions.
In contrast, our NRMF consistently and significantly outperforms both baselines and prior state-of-the-art methods. This demonstrates the effectiveness of our approach in modeling motion dynamics: by jointly incorporating pose, transition, and acceleration priors, NRMF achieves improved pose accuracy while producing more temporally coherent and physically plausible motions, and successfully mitigates error accumulation. Qualitative results in <a href="#S5.F5" title="In Motion estimation from 3D observations ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Supp. Mat. further support these observations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/qua.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/571;" width="685" height="571" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Qualitative comparison with state-of-the-art methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> on motion estimation from <span id="S5.F5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#5E7B8B;">noisy partial</span> observation (top two) and motion in-between (bottom two) where the <span id="S5.F5.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BFA2D3;">purple body parts</span> represent the recovered motion. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motion refinement for human mesh recovery</h4>

<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ftting to 2D obsertations on 3DPW. We compare the refinement results after optimizing SMPLer-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> with different prior terms. MPJPE and MPVPE are in millimeters.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T3.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:113.9pt;vertical-align:-54.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-42.8pt,9.8pt) scale(0.852917291702034,0.852917291702034) ;">
