</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our motion prior can also be used with regression-based human pose estimation methods as an efficient optimization-based refinement module for mesh recovery from videos. <a href="#S5.T3" title="In Motion refinement for human mesh recovery ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares our method with the SotA baselines of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> using SMPLer-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> as initialization.
Notably, while optimization improves pixel alignment, it does not guarantee plausible motion due to the lack of constraints and biased optimization without appropriate priors.
Specifically, only applying a pose prior (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-order) usually leads to large errors in transition and acceleration due to the absence of higher-order motion constraints. Notably, the ablations highlight the significance of each prior term separately.
By integrating acceleration and transition, we improve the Acc Err and Trans Err significantly. Applying our <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NRMF(full)</span> model for refinement will boost the per-frame mesh reconstruction accuracy while preserving plausible and realistic motion transitions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motion estimation from RGB(-D) in-the-wild observations</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Learning Riemannian Motion Fields (NRMF) ‣ 3 Neural Riemannian Motion Fields ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, our method is readily applicable to real-world cases, enabling robust motion recovery from various modalities including in-the-wild RGB(-D) videos, <em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span> partial and noisy 2D &amp; 3D observations. We conduct our evaluation on i3DB (RGB), EgoBody (RGB) and PROX (RGB &amp; RGB-D) datasets. Specifically, we leverage the off-the-shelf 2D pose estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, human mask segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, along with a plane detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> model as observations during optimization following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.
As shown in <a href="#S5.T5" title="In Motion Generation and In-betweening ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tabs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Motion Generation and In-betweening ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Motion Generation and In-betweening ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, conventional pose priors perform reasonably well on visible joints but struggle in occluded regions. Relying solely on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-order pose information leads them to overfit visible joints, producing temporally inconsistent and physically implausible reconstructions. In contrast, NRMF incorporates richer dynamics through transition and acceleration priors, yielding substantially lower position and acceleration errors on both visible and occluded joints compared to existing motion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> and pose <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> priors. Qualitative comparisons in <a href="#S5.F6" title="In Motion estimation from RGB(-D) in-the-wild observations ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> highlight our method’s superior motion recovery, while <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Test-time optimization ‣ 4 NRMF as Versatile Generative Priors ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that the pose and transition distributions learned by NRMF better match the ground-truth distribution than those of prior models. We provide more details in our supplementary material as well as more in-the-wild qualitative results in the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF00FF;">supplementary videos</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/qua_small.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/625;" width="685" height="625" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Qualitative comparison with state-of-the-art methods for in-the-wild motion estimation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
