<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For motion generation, we follow the protocol of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, sampling 50 motion sequences (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\mathrm{sec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sec</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\mathrm{sec}</annotation></semantics></math> each) for each initial state. As shown in <a href="#S5.T4" title="In Motion Generation and In-betweening ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, VPoser achieves the lowest FID and APD scores, but produces less diverse and expressive motions. In contrast, DPoser yields high diversity (APD) at the cost of low plausibility (FID). NRDF strikes a balance between fidelity and diversity. Among the motion priors, our method achieves the best overall FID for motion while maintaining competitive APD, indicating that NRMF generates realistic and diverse motions. Additionally, NRMF produces the lowest mean acceleration norm, reflecting stable motion with reduced jitter.
For in-betweening, we follow a similar protocol to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, focusing on sparse keyframe supervision and infilling refinement. Specifically, we provide a sparse set of keyframe observations corresponding either to the two fixed endpoints, to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the total frames, or to a <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math> scheme divided across the start, middle, and end of the sequence similar to the protocol in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. We compare our method against <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and SLERP. Our method explicitly leverages high-order motion dynamics, enabling robust interpolation even under extremely sparse constraints. <a href="#S5.F5" title="In Motion estimation from 3D observations ‣ 5.2 Evaluation &amp; Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates a qualitative comparison in the two-endpoint setting, where our method produces more natural and plausible interpolations. Additional qualitative results, as well as quantitative evaluations across different sparsity levels, are provided in the supplementary materials.</p>
</div>
