<div id="p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering">
</div>
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:144%;">Supplementary Material 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">A </span>Geometry of Articulated Motions</h2>

<div id="S1a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the sake of a self-contained manuscript, we now provide a complete treatment of the Riemannian geometry of rotations that we use in this work. For completeness, we also recall the definitions we provide in the main paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Riemannian manifolds</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we define an <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Riemannian manifold</span>, embedded in an ambient Euclidean space <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and endowed with a <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Riemannian metric</span> <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}\triangleq(\mathbf{G}_{\mathbf{x}})_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>≜</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}\triangleq(\mathbf{G}_{\mathbf{x}})_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a smooth curved space <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M},\mathbf{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M},\mathbf{G})</annotation></semantics></math>. A vector <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tangent</em> to <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">iff</em> there exists a smooth curve <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:[0,1]\to\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:[0,1]\to\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> s.t. <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(0)=\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(0)=\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\gamma}(0)=\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\gamma}(0)=\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. The velocities of all such curves through <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> form the <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tangent space</em> <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}=\{\dot{\gamma}(0)\,|\,\gamma:\mathbb{R}\to\mathcal{M}\text{ is smooth around $0$ and }\gamma(0)=\mathbf{x}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mtext> is smooth around </mtext><mn>0</mn><mtext> and </mtext></mrow><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{M}=\{\dot{\gamma}(0)\,|\,\gamma:\mathbb{R}\to\mathcal{M}\text{ is smooth around $0$ and }\gamma(0)=\mathbf{x}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will also work with a product of <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> manifolds, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1:K}\vcentcolon=\mathcal{M}_{1}\times\mathcal{M}_{2}\times\dots\times\mathcal{M}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1:K}\vcentcolon=\mathcal{M}_{1}\times\mathcal{M}_{2}\times\dots\times\mathcal{M}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, For identical manifolds, <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text"></span> <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{i}\equiv\mathcal{M}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{i}\equiv\mathcal{M}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we recover the <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">power manifold</em>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{K}\vcentcolon=\mathcal{M}_{1:K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{K}\vcentcolon=\mathcal{M}_{1:K}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this work, we will specifically work on the differentiable manifold of a product of <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> rotations, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdfn4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmdfn4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmdfn4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The manifold of 3D rotations admit the structure:</span></p>
<table id="S1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}:\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}=\mathbf{I},\operatorname{det}(\mathbf{R})=1\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>det</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}:\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}=\mathbf{I},\operatorname{det}(\mathbf{R})=1\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To characterize this manifold, we start by deriving its tangent space:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdfn5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Tangent space of <math id="Thmdfn5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmdfn5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tangent space of <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined throughout as the <em id="Thmdfn5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">left-invariant vector fields</em>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\,:\,\bm{\Omega}\in\mathfrak{so}(3)\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\,:\,\bm{\Omega}\in\mathfrak{so}(3)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{so}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{so}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the set of <em id="Thmdfn5.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">skew-symmetric</em> tensors:</span></p>
<table id="S1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{so}(3)=\left\{\bm{\Omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}:\bm{\Omega}^{\top}=-\bm{\Omega}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{so}(3)=\left\{\bm{\Omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}:\bm{\Omega}^{\top}=-\bm{\Omega}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Omega}\mathbf{h}=\bm{\omega}\times\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝛚</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐡</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Omega}\mathbf{h}=\bm{\omega}\times\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="Thmdfn5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="Thmdfn5.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">axial</em> or <em id="Thmdfn5.p1.3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">rotation vector</em>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Differentiating the constraints that specify <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> leads us to the definition of skew-symmetric matrices:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R})}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}=\dot{\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\mathbf{R}+\mathbf{R}^{\top}\dot{\mathbf{R}}=0\implies\bm{\Omega}=-\bm{\Omega}^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R})}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}=\dot{\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\mathbf{R}+\mathbf{R}^{\top}\dot{\mathbf{R}}=0\implies\bm{\Omega}=-\bm{\Omega}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Omega}=\mathbf{R}^{\top}\dot{\mathbf{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Omega}=\mathbf{R}^{\top}\dot{\mathbf{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a skew symmetric matrix and the vector <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathbf{R}}=\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathbf{R}}=\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is tangent to <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To work with functions on this manifold, for example in optimization, we will further introduce the notions of differentials and gradients that makes intrinsic sense.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6</span></span><span id="Thmdfn6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Riemannian gradient for <math id="Thmdfn6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmdfn6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Riemannian (or intrinsic) gradient of a function defined on <math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique tangent vector satisfying:</span></p>
<table id="S1.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathrm{grad}f(\mathbf{R}),\xi\rangle_{R}=Df(\mathbf{R})[\bm{\xi}]=\langle\nabla f(\mathbf{R}),\bm{\xi}\rangle\quad" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>grad</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝝃</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathrm{grad}f(\mathbf{R}),\xi\rangle_{R}=Df(\mathbf{R})[\bm{\xi}]=\langle\nabla f(\mathbf{R}),\bm{\xi}\rangle\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmdfn6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\xi}\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛏</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\xi}\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The intrinsic gradient is the only part of the gradient that makes sense to a local citizen. For <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>, given the Euclidean gradient, it can be obtained via the <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{egrad2rgrad}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>egrad2rgrad</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{egrad2rgrad}</annotation></semantics></math> operator:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop1a" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop1a.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmprop1a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmprop1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{egrad2rgrad}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>egrad2rgrad</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{egrad2rgrad}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmprop1a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmprop1a.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop1a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop1a.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The projection of the Euclidean gradient <math id="Thmprop1a.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f(\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f(\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> onto the tangent space of <math id="Thmprop1a.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the Riemannian gradient:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{grad}f(\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>grad</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{grad}f(\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\triangleq\Pi_{\mathbf{R}}\big(\nabla f(\mathbf{R})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\triangleq\Pi_{\mathbf{R}}\big(\nabla f(\mathbf{R})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\,\skewrm\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mpadded style="width:2.059em;" width="2.059em"><mi>skew</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\,\skewrm\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{R}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f(\mathbf{R})-\nabla f(\mathbf{R})^{\top}\mathbf{R}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{R}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f(\mathbf{R})-\nabla f(\mathbf{R})^{\top}\mathbf{R}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop1a.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1a.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmprop1a.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathbf{R}}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathbf{R}}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthogonal projector onto the tangent space of <math id="Thmprop1a.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is famously known as the <math id="Thmprop1a.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{egrad2rgrad}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>egrad2rgrad</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{egrad2rgrad}</annotation></semantics></math> in various packages including ManOpt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">In a differential geometric setting, any vector can be written down as a linear combination of normal and tangential components.
To project the Euclidean gradient onto the tangent space
<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\,:\,\bm{\Omega}\in\mathfrak{so}(3)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)=\left\{\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\,:\,\bm{\Omega}\in\mathfrak{so}(3)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, our aim will be to <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">kill</em> the normal component, leaving only the tangential one.
Let us start by writing:</span></p>
<table id="S1.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)=\mathbf{R}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)=\mathbf{R}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since any square matrix can uniquely be written as the sum of a symmetric and a skew-symmetric matrix, we can write:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\sym\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)+\mathbf{R}\skewrm\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mpadded style="width:1.703em;" width="1.703em"><mi>sym</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mpadded style="width:2.059em;" width="2.059em"><mi>skew</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\sym\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)+\mathbf{R}\skewrm\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">By definition of the tangent space, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}\skewrm(\mathbf{v})\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.059em;" width="2.059em"><mi>skew</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\skewrm(\mathbf{v})\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Also note that <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{R}\sym\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mpadded style="width:1.703em;" width="1.703em"><mi>sym</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\sym\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> lives in the <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normal space</em>, <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, it is orthogonal to all tangent vectors with respect to the Frobenius inner product. To see this, let <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{S}=\sym\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:1.703em;" width="1.703em"><mi>sym</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}=\sym\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and write:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle\mathbf{R}\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑𝐒</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle\mathbf{R}\mathbf{S},\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\left(\mathbf{R}\mathbf{S}\right)^{\top}\left(\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑𝐒</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\left(\mathbf{R}\mathbf{S}\right)^{\top}\left(\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf{S}^{\top}\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf{S}^{\top}\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf{S}^{\top}\bm{\Omega}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf{S}^{\top}\bm{\Omega}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">Note once again that <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> by definition.
To see the last equality, consider</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})=\mathrm{Tr}\left((\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})^{\top}\right)=\mathrm{Tr}((-\bm{\Omega})\mathbf{S})=-\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐒</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})=\mathrm{Tr}\left((\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})^{\top}\right)=\mathrm{Tr}((-\bm{\Omega})\mathbf{S})=-\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">Observe that <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})=-\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})\implies\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})=-\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})\implies\mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{S}\bm{\Omega})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, because <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{R}\sym\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mpadded style="width:1.703em;" width="1.703em"><mi>sym</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\sym\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, it vanishes under projection onto the tangent space and we are left with the skew component, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{R}\skewrm\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mpadded style="width:2.059em;" width="2.059em"><mi>skew</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}\skewrm\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to the following orthogonal projection:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{grad}f(\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>grad</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{grad}f(\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\triangleq\Pi_{\mathbf{R}}\big(\nabla f(\mathbf{R})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≜</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\triangleq\Pi_{\mathbf{R}}\big(\nabla f(\mathbf{R})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\skewrm\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mpadded style="width:2.059em;" width="2.059em"><mi>skew</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\skewrm\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\nabla f\left(\mathbf{R}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">This concludes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we will introduce the exponential and logarithmic maps that are required to <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">walk</em> on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>. Most of the proofs, which we will omit for brevity, are based on the properties of the <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">matrix exponential</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7</span></span><span id="Thmdfn7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exponential Map (<math id="Thmdfn7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathrm{{Exp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathrm{{Exp}})</annotation></semantics></math>))<span id="Thmdfn7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\xi}\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)=\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛏</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\xi}\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)=\mathbf{R}\bm{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> denote any tangent vector with <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Omega}=[\bm{\omega}]_{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝛚</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Omega}=[\bm{\omega}]_{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>. The exponential map at <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a map <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{{Exp}}:\mathrm{SO}(3)\times\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)\to\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{{Exp}}:\mathrm{SO}(3)\times\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)\to\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\mathbf{R}}(\bm{\Omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Exp</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\mathbf{R}}(\bm{\Omega})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\mathrm{{Exp}}(\bm{\Omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Exp</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\mathrm{{Exp}}(\bm{\Omega})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\left(\mathbf{I}+\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}\bm{\Omega}+\frac{1-\cos(\theta)}{\theta}\bm{\Omega}^{2}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝛀</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{R}\left(\mathbf{I}+\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\theta}\bm{\Omega}+\frac{1-\cos(\theta)}{\theta}\bm{\Omega}^{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\|\omega\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\|\omega\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdfn7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{{Exp}}(\bm{\Omega})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{{Exp}}(\bm{\Omega})</annotation></semantics></math> (right hand side) is known as the celebrated <span id="Thmdfn7.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rodrigues’ formula</span>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8</span></span><span id="Thmdfn8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Logarithmic Map (<math id="Thmdfn8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathrm{{Log}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Log</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathrm{{Log}})</annotation></semantics></math>))<span id="Thmdfn8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The inverse of <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{{Exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Exp</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{{Exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the logarithmic map <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{{Log}}:\mathrm{SO}(3)\times\mathrm{SO}(3)\to\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{{Log}}:\mathrm{SO}(3)\times\mathrm{SO}(3)\to\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>, which expresses any rotation <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> in the tangent space <math id="Thmdfn8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{{Log}}_{\mathbf{R}}(\mathbf{R}_{1})=\mathrm{{Log}}(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Log</mi><mi>𝐑</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{{Log}}_{\mathbf{R}}(\mathbf{R}_{1})=\mathrm{{Log}}(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\frac{\theta}{2\sin(\theta)}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}_{1}-\mathbf{R}_{1}^{\top}\mathbf{R}\right),&amp;\theta\in(\pi,\pi)\setminus\{0\}\\
\frac{1}{2}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}_{1}-\mathbf{R}_{1}^{\top}\mathbf{R}\right),&amp;\theta=0,\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\frac{\theta}{2\sin(\theta)}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}_{1}-\mathbf{R}_{1}^{\top}\mathbf{R}\right),&amp;\theta\in(\pi,\pi)\setminus\{0\}\\
\frac{1}{2}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}_{1}-\mathbf{R}_{1}^{\top}\mathbf{R}\right),&amp;\theta=0,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\theta:=d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}(\mathbf{R},\mathbf{R}_{1})=\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}_{1}\right)-1}{2}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta:=d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}(\mathbf{R},\mathbf{R}_{1})=\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathbf{R}^{\top}\mathbf{R}_{1}\right)-1}{2}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn8.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn8.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is known as the <span id="Thmdfn8.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">geodesic distance</span> between two rotations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach benefits from the dynamics on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> through the angular velocity and acceleration, which are the backbone of geometric mechanics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. We will derive the basic notions below and refer the reader these references for a broader exposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9</span></span><span id="Thmdfn9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Angular velocity)<span id="Thmdfn9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">While any tangent vector can be interpreted as representing an <em id="Thmdfn9.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">angular velocity</em><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">4</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">with an appropriate choice of the body-fixed frame</span></span></span></span>, it is useful to represent the velocity of the geodesic motion on <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> as a time (<math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>) dependent curve <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(t)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\Omega}}(t)=R^{\top}(t)\dot{R}(t)\&gt;\in\mathfrak{so}(3)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\Omega}}(t)=R^{\top}(t)\dot{R}(t)\&gt;\in\mathfrak{so}(3),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the corresponding <em id="Thmdfn9.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">angular velocity matrix</em> <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Omega}}:={{\Omega}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Omega}}:={{\Omega}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is skew-symmetric.
Since <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{so}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{so}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, we instead store the <span id="Thmdfn9.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">angular velocity vector</span>, <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the skew–symmetric <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> via the <span id="Thmdfn9.p1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hat-operator</span>: <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\bm{\omega}}={\bm{\Omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\bm{\omega}}={\bm{\Omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\omega(t)}={{\Omega}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\omega(t)}={{\Omega}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and equivalently <math id="Thmdfn9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\bm{\omega}]_{x}={\bm{\Omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝛚</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\bm{\omega}]_{x}={\bm{\Omega}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/angular_acc.png" id="S1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/450;" width="685" height="450" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S1.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of angular velocity and accelaration for two points on a manifold. Given two points on the manifold (<math id="S1.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), the velocities are vectors in the tangent planes at those points. In practice, to compute an angular acceleration, they are <em id="S1.F7.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parallel transported</em> to the tangent space of the identity and then compared. If the points are close enough, the variation of the tangent spaces are ignored, and the computation is carried out by directly comparing vectors. <em id="S1.F7.6.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sphere is chosen for illustration purposes whereas the manifold or articulated bodies is higher dimensional.</em></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmprop2a" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop2a.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmprop2a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Angular acceleration)<span id="Thmprop2a.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop2a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop2a.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop2a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The time-derivative of angular velocity defines the angular acceleration:</span></p>
<table id="S1.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}[{\bm{\omega}}_{t}]_{x}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}:=[\dot{\bm{\omega}}_{t}]_{x}:=\ddot{\mathbf{R}}_{t}=\mathbf{R}_{t}\left({\bm{\Omega}}_{t}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}[{\bm{\omega}}_{t}]_{x}}{\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}t}:=[\dot{\bm{\omega}}_{t}]_{x}:=\ddot{\mathbf{R}}_{t}=\mathbf{R}_{t}\left({\bm{\Omega}}_{t}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop2a.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop2a.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmprop2a.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also skew-symmetric.
The additional <math id="Thmprop2a.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Omega}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝛀</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Omega}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> term accounts for the curvature of the configuration space when transforming to the inertial frame.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We start by differentiating the angular velocity in <a href="#S1.E35" title="In Definition 9 (Angular velocity). ‣ Riemannian manifolds ‣ A Geometry of Articulated Motions ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a> to obtain:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ddot{R}(t)=\dot{R}(t)\Omega(t)+R(t)\dot{\Omega}(t)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ddot{R}(t)=\dot{R}(t)\Omega(t)+R(t)\dot{\Omega}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{R}(t)=R(t)\Omega(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{R}(t)=R(t)\Omega(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (by <a href="#S1.E35" title="In Definition 9 (Angular velocity). ‣ Riemannian manifolds ‣ A Geometry of Articulated Motions ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>), we obtain:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ddot{R}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ddot{R}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={R}(t)\Omega^{2}(t)+R(t)\dot{\Omega}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={R}(t)\Omega^{2}(t)+R(t)\dot{\Omega}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={R}\left(t\right)\left(\Omega^{2}\left(t\right)+\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={R}\left(t\right)\left(\Omega^{2}\left(t\right)+\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">Left multiplying this with <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(t)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(t)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> yields:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(t)^{\top}\ddot{R}(t)=\Omega^{2}\left(t\right)+\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(t)^{\top}\ddot{R}(t)=\Omega^{2}\left(t\right)+\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since the square of any skew–symmetric matrix is symmetric, this is a natural decomposition of a matrix into its symmetric and skew-symmetric parts. Therefore to recover <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as the angular acceleration, we take the skew:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\dot{\bm{\omega}}(t)}=\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\dot{\bm{\omega}}(t)}=\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\skewrm\left(\Omega^{2}\left(t\right)+\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.059em;" width="2.059em"><mi>skew</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\skewrm\left(\Omega^{2}\left(t\right)+\dot{\Omega}\left(t\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.E42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\skewrm\left(R\left(t\right)^{\top}\ddot{R}\left(t\right)\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.059em;" width="2.059em"><mi>skew</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\skewrm\left(R\left(t\right)^{\top}\ddot{R}\left(t\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.5.1" class="ltx_text">where the last equality follows from <a href="#S1.E40" title="In Proof. ‣ Riemannian manifolds ‣ A Geometry of Articulated Motions ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>. In matrix form, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\dot{\bm{\omega}}]_{x}={\bm{\Omega}}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}}=\mathrm{\mathbf{R}^{\top}\ddot{\mathbf{R}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝛀</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\dot{\bm{\omega}}]_{x}={\bm{\Omega}}^{2}+\dot{{\bm{\Omega}}}=\mathrm{\mathbf{R}^{\top}\ddot{\mathbf{R}}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\mathbf{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\mathbf{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the second derivative in <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ambient space</em>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(On Angular Acceleration)<span id="Thmremark4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In practice, we use a second-order finite differencing scheme for angular acceleration. While being more efficient than using the logarithmic maps to compare different velocities in the same tangent frame (through parallel transport as illustrated in <a href="#S1.F7" title="In Riemannian manifolds ‣ A Geometry of Articulated Motions ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), it might also be less accurate. Thus, we compare the two disceretization schemes in <a href="#S1.F8" title="In Riemannian manifolds ‣ A Geometry of Articulated Motions ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, revealing that the two schemes are quite close in practice. This motivates us to leverage Euclidean central differencing, after estimating the angular velocities.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09667v1/ang_log_central.svg" id="S1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/512;" width="685" height="512"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S1.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparing <math id="S1.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math>-central differencing to classical central differencing for angular acceleration estimation. The attained error between the two (last row) is relatively low indicating that simple central differences are good estimators of angular acceleration, given geometrically meaningful angular velocities.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Extension to power manifolds</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As articulated bodies are represented as a collection of rotations, we consider the power manifold (product of identical manifolds):</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 10</span></span><span id="Thmdfn10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Geodesic distances of articulated bodies)<span id="Thmdfn10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Endowed with the <math id="Thmdfn10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="Thmdfn10.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">product metric</em>, the geodesic distances of articulated bodies can be written as:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\bm{\theta},\bm{\theta}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\bm{\theta},\bm{\theta}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\|d(\mathbf{R}_{1},\mathbf{R}_{1}^{\prime}),d(\mathbf{R}_{2},\mathbf{R}_{2}^{\prime}),\dots,d(\mathbf{R}_{K},\mathbf{R}_{K}^{\prime})\|_{p}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|d(\mathbf{R}_{1},\mathbf{R}_{1}^{\prime}),d(\mathbf{R}_{2},\mathbf{R}_{2}^{\prime}),\dots,d(\mathbf{R}_{K},\mathbf{R}_{K}^{\prime})\|_{p},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdfn10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{i}\in\bm{\theta}\in\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{i}\in\bm{\theta}\in\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdfn10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}_{i}^{\prime}\in\bm{\theta}^{\prime}\in\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}_{i}^{\prime}\in\bm{\theta}^{\prime}\in\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this work, we use <math id="Thmdfn10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdfn10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\equiv d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\equiv d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 11</span></span><span id="Thmdfn11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exp / Log map on power manifold)<span id="Thmdfn11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The natural isomorphism allows us to write its exponential map <math id="Thmdfn11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathcal{T}_{\bm{\theta}}\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}\to\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Exp</mi><mi>𝛉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>𝛉</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\bm{\theta}}:\mathcal{T}_{\bm{\theta}}\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}\to\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> component-wise:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\bm{\theta}}=\left(\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\mathbf{R}_{1}},\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\mathbf{R}_{2}},\dots,\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\mathbf{R}_{K}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Exp</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Exp</mi><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Exp</mi><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Exp</mi><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\bm{\theta}}=\left(\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\mathbf{R}_{1}},\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\mathbf{R}_{2}},\dots,\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\mathbf{R}_{K}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdfn11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Akin to this, is the logarithmic map, <math id="Thmdfn11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{{Log}}_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Log</mi><mi>𝛉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{{Log}}_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdfn12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_dfn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdfn12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 12</span></span><span id="Thmdfn12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Riemannian gradient for an articulated pose)<span id="Thmdfn12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdfn12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdfn12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdfn12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since the tangent spaces and therefore <math id="Thmdfn12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>𝛉</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> are replicas, the gradient of a smooth function <math id="Thmdfn12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathrm{SO}(3)^{K}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. <math id="Thmdfn12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is also the Cartesian product of the individual gradients:</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{grad}_{\bm{\theta}}{f(\bm{\theta})}=\left(\mathrm{grad}_{\mathbf{R}_{1}}{f(\bm{\theta})},\,\dots,\,\mathrm{grad}_{\mathbf{R}_{K}}{f(\bm{\theta})}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>grad</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>grad</mi><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>grad</mi><msub><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{grad}_{\bm{\theta}}{f(\bm{\theta})}=\left(\mathrm{grad}_{\mathbf{R}_{1}}{f(\bm{\theta})},\,\dots,\,\mathrm{grad}_{\mathbf{R}_{K}}{f(\bm{\theta})}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B </span>Algorithms, Architecture &amp; Details</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pseudocode of RMF-Integrator</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the pseudocode for integration of motion via porjection onto the learned manifold in <a href="#alg1" title="In Pseudocode of RMF-Integrator ‣ B Algorithms, Architecture &amp; Details ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> RMF Integrator</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            An initial pose <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and velocity <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, a possibly noisy acceleration sequence <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and all components of the trained network <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence the projectors <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math>),

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Plausible motion <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leftarrow 1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leftarrow 1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg1.l3.5" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Euler integrate of velocities
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\leftarrow\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t-1}+\lambda_{t}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\leftarrow\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t-1}+\lambda_{t}\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\leftarrow\Pi^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>ω</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}\leftarrow\Pi^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leftarrow 1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leftarrow 1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg1.l7.5" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Update poses
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{t}\leftarrow\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\bm{\theta}_{t-1}}\bigl(\alpha_{t}\,[{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}_{t-1}]_{x}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Exp</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{t}\leftarrow\mathrm{{Exp}}_{\bm{\theta}_{t-1}}\bigl(\alpha_{t}\,[{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}_{t-1}]_{x}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{t}\leftarrow\Pi^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{t}\leftarrow\Pi^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
            Compose <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from individual estimates <math id="alg1.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t},\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t},\bm{\theta}_{t}\}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{t},\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t},\bm{\theta}_{t}\}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/mechas3.png" id="S2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/161;" width="685" height="161" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="S2.F9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Transitions and accelerations overlaid onto the pose distributions.<span id="S2.F9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Four motions originating from the most common body pose in the AMASS dataset are represented as colored arrows on the surface of the spheres depicting the per-joint pose distributions comprising <math id="S2.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>. Spheres here are oriented to maximize visibility of the high-probability region of the distribution rather than showcasing the range of motions through skeletal alignment. Each transition arrow is split into different segments representing the transition between consecutive time-steps.The color of the arrows maps the three components of the angular acceleration to RGB space.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2a" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Parametric model</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ the SMPL+H body model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, consistent with the AMASS dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> on which our work is based. Since our focus is exclusively on modeling body motion, we follow HuMoR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> tp exclude hand joints from consideration, retaining only the 22 body joints (including the root). Incorporating hand joints into the optimization alongside body motion is straightforward, which lies outside the scope of our present study but can be a future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Test-time optimization</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement our optimizer and optimization pipeline with Pytorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. We provide full details of our HuMoR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>-inspired test-time-optimization in this section. During runtime, given a sequence of 2D or 3D data observations, <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span> joint detections, point clouds, described as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{O}}_{0:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐎</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{O}}_{0:T}</annotation></semantics></math>, our goal is to recover the motion sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{X}_{t}\}_{t=0}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> that aligns with the observations while being <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">likely</em> under the NRFM.
Our mutlti-stage optimization starts from a proper initialization using the pose prior, by minimizing the following combined objective:</p>
<table id="S2.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{I}(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}+\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}+\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}+\lambda_{reg}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{I}(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}+\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}+\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}+\lambda_{reg}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}=\|\bm{\beta}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}=\|\bm{\beta}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the shape prior term. <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}=f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}=f_{\Phi}^{R}(\bm{\theta}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is our pose prior term predicting the distance to the nearest plausible pose<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Note that for VPoser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}=\|\mathbf{z}\|^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}=\|\mathbf{z}\|^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is the latent code.</span></span></span>. Moreover, we apply additional regularization terms where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{smooth}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{smooth}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{bl}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>smooth</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>smooth</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>bl</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>bl</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{smooth}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{smooth}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{bl}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bl}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{smooth}}=\sum_{t=0}^{T}\|\bm{J}_{t+1}-\bm{J}_{t}\|^{2},\quad\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bl}}=\sum_{t=0}^{T}\|\mathbf{B}_{t+1}-\mathbf{B}_{t}\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>smooth</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>bl</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{smooth}}=\sum_{t=0}^{T}\|\bm{J}_{t+1}-\bm{J}_{t}\|^{2},\quad\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bl}}=\sum_{t=0}^{T}\|\mathbf{B}_{t+1}-\mathbf{B}_{t}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> are the bone length of the human body. These regularization terms essentially enforces the skeleton to be consistent over time and prevent large jitters. Finally, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the task-dependent data terms and will be different when given different types of inputs. Specifically, in the task of fitting to 2D joints the objective function is written as:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}^{\mathrm{2D}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{data}}\sum_{t=0}^{T}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\sigma_{t}^{j}\rho(\Pi(\bm{J}_{t}^{j})-{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑱</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}^{\mathrm{2D}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{data}}\sum_{t=0}^{T}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\sigma_{t}^{j}\rho(\Pi(\bm{J}_{t}^{j})-{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the 2D joint observations with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as the confidence score for 2D joint observations. <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is the robust Geman-McClure function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the 2D re-projection with the 3D joints <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{J}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from human meshes. When fitting 3D joints, the objective becomes:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}^{\mathrm{3D}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{data}}\sum_{t=0}^{T}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\|\bm{J}_{t}^{j}-{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{j}\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>J</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑱</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}^{\mathrm{3D}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{data}}\sum_{t=0}^{T}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\|\bm{J}_{t}^{j}-{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{j}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the 3D joint observations. <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be further extended to various observation representations such as 3D point clouds:</p>
<table id="S2.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}^{\mathrm{PC3D}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{data}}\sum_{t=0}^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{t}}w_{\text{bs}}\min_{\bm{V}_{t}\in V_{t}}\left\|\bm{V}_{t}-{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{i}\right\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi><mi>PC3D</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>bs</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐎</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}^{\mathrm{PC3D}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{data}}\sum_{t=0}^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{t}}w_{\text{bs}}\min_{\bm{V}_{t}\in V_{t}}\left\|\bm{V}_{t}-{\mathbf{O}}_{t}^{i}\right\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{O}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{O}}</annotation></semantics></math> now represents 3D human point cloud observations extracted from depth images with person segmentation masks obtained from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\text{bs}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mtext>bs</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\text{bs}}</annotation></semantics></math> a bisquare weight <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> based on the Chamfer distance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While optimizing <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> yields refined estimates <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, it cannot combat motion ambiguities since the pose prior only captures <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0^{th}</annotation></semantics></math>-order distribution. After initialization, we factor in our transition and acceleration priors as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}:=f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})\quad\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}:=f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}:=f_{\Psi}^{\omega}(\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})\quad\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}:=f_{\Xi}^{\dot{\omega}}(\ddot{\bm{\theta}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Disentangle and model global motion representation</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For our motion state representation <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we explicitly disentangle global motion (relative to the current state) <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> from the local pose configuration for each state. Specifically, we achieve this by multiplying the inverse root transform with each pose, mapping all poses into a local coordinate frame aligned to a canonical facing direction. This canonicalization simplifies the learning process by removing unnecessary global variation. Inspired by prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, we predict <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}}\ ,c_{t}^{i}\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}}\ ,c_{t}^{i}\},</annotation></semantics></math> through the sub-module network integrated within the transition prior framework based on the same rich contextual input following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>. During optimization, it is constrained by data terms during the optimization. Additionally, we expand <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}</annotation></semantics></math> to account also for floor contact physics constraint terms (contact loss <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{contact}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>contact</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{contact}}</annotation></semantics></math> and contact height loss <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ch}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ch</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ch}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S2.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{contact}}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{contact}}\lambda_{\text{cj}}c_{t}^{i}\left\|\mathbf{j}_{t}^{i}-\mathbf{j}_{t-1}^{i}\right\|^{2}+\lambda_{\text{cv}}c_{t}^{i}\left\|\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}^{i}\right\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>contact</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>cj</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐣</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐣</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>cv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{contact}}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{contact}}\lambda_{\text{cj}}c_{t}^{i}\left\|\mathbf{j}_{t}^{i}-\mathbf{j}_{t-1}^{i}\right\|^{2}+\lambda_{\text{cv}}c_{t}^{i}\left\|\dot{\bm{\theta}}_{t}^{i}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.E49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ch}}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{contact}}\lambda_{\text{ch}}c_{t}^{i}\max(|\mathbf{j}_{z,t}^{i}|-\delta,0)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ch</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>ch</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐣</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ch}}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{contact}}\lambda_{\text{ch}}c_{t}^{i}\max(|\mathbf{j}_{z,t}^{i}|-\delta,0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the contact probability predicted by the transition prior network specifically for joint <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at timestep <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and the contact height loss will further constrain the z-component of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{j}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐣</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{j}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The updated objective function then becomes:</p>
<table id="S2.E50" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E50X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E50X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{I}^{\prime}(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}+\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}+\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{I}^{\prime}(\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{r}},\bm{\theta},\bm{\beta})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{data}}+\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\beta}}+\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E50Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E50Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}+\lambda_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}+\lambda_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\lambda_{\mathrm{reg}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{reg}}+\lambda_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}\mathcal{L}_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}+\lambda_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}\mathcal{L}_{\ddot{\bm{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we apply the rollout in <a href="#alg1" title="In Pseudocode of RMF-Integrator ‣ B Algorithms, Architecture &amp; Details ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> within each iteration to update the motion state <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> for drift correction and rebuild the consistent state. During the test-time optimization of motion estimation, we balance the loss terms and prior with the following coefficients: <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{2d}}=1e^{-3},\lambda_{\mathrm{smooth}}=10,\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}=8e^{-2},\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}=8e^{-2},\lambda_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}=1.0,\lambda_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}=5e^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>smooth</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜷</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{2d}}=1e^{-3},\lambda_{\mathrm{smooth}}=10,\lambda_{\bm{\beta}}=8e^{-2},\lambda_{\bm{\theta}}=8e^{-2},\lambda_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}=1.0,\lambda_{\dot{\bm{\theta}}}=5e^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter terms influence the trust in each prior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training data generation</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now provide more details of our training process, including the data preparation. For training dataset, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> to use the same split and clip the motion sequence to extract the middle 80% and downsample the dataset to 30 fps. We then sample the negative training samples with an adopted multi-step mechanism for querying the nearest neighbours using FAISS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> to speed up the NN process. To accelerate nearest-neighbor (NN) search, we adopt a two-stage retrieval scheme similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. In the first stage, we use FAISS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> to retrieve <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coarse candidates for each noisy transition or acceleration. In the second stage, we refine these candidates by computing the distance and selecting the closest <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> neighbors. In our setup, we set <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike Pose-NDF, which averages the distances to the top 5 nearest neighbors, we determine the final match using only the single closest neighbor. To generate negative samples, we adopt a mixed sampling strategy with fixed ratios for three types of negatives: distance-targeted half-Gaussian distribution based perturbations (60%) following the scheme in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, random-swap candidates (30%), and fully random candidates (10%).
Specifically, for the first category, we perturb the ground-truth transition or acceleration by applying a small random rotation sampled from a zero-mean Gaussian distribution, thereby introducing controlled manifold noise.
For the second category, we randomly select transitions from the clean motion dataset without ensuring semantic proximity to the query.
For the third category, we generate fully random quaternions and normalize them to unit length.
All negative candidate are concatenated and passed through a single FAISS-based nearest neighbor search to compute their distances to the training set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training details</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we introduce our baselines HuMoR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, RoHM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and MDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> which were trained on the same split as ours for a fair comparison. Specifically, the original MDM essentially requires clean motion because it would replace denoised joints
with visible input joints at each timestep of denoising and was not trained on AMASS. To make it comparable on AMASS, we train MDM with the same split and with quaternion as the rotation representation instead of the original <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="hml_{vec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">hml_{vec}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a high-dimensional composition vector of motion states. For PhaseMP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, we use the official code for evaluation and training. Finally, our prior modules (T-NRDF and A-NRDF) are pre-trained independently in the first stage and jointly optimized in the second stage with learning rate of 3e-5. For training the global motion prediction layers, we follow a simple scheme to train it jointly with the network in our experiments. We choose the checkpoints by selecting the best-performing model on validation set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/qua_supp_0.png" id="S2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/592;" width="685" height="592" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="S2.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Qualitative results on in-the-wild fitting on PROX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> dataset. We compare our method agains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, which demonstrates the robustness of our method under challenging occlusion.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/qua_supp_1_cropped.png" id="S2.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/367;" width="685" height="367" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F11.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="S2.F11.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative results on downstream applications. The <span id="S2.F11.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#5E7B8B;">mesh and body parts</span> are the observation inputs and we show the <span id="S2.F11.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BFA2D3;">output or optimization results</span>. For in-the-wild fitting, we conduct experiments on both in-door datasets (bottom row) and online videos (upper row).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09667v1/qua_supp_2.png" id="S2.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/335;" width="685" height="335" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="S2.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">In-the-wild motion estimation. We show qualitative comparisons on real-world videos with challenging conditions such as occlusion, background clutter. Our method recovers more plausible, consistent, and anatomically accurate motions. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Details on joint distribution visualizations</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although poses are defined in the product manifold of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}\in\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}\in\mathrm{SO}(3)^{N_{J}-1}</annotation></semantics></math>), for the purpose of visualization, we separately analyze each joint angle space. We observe that joint rotations can be plotted onto the surface of a sphere by rotating skeletal bones in their rest position. Precisely, given a unit vector representing the direction of the axis between two consecutive joints in the kinematic tree, we rotate it using the Rodrigues’ rotation formula and obtain a rotated unit vector. Repeating this procedure for a set of joint poses, we obtain a set of points on a sphere. We thus fit these points using a spherical kernel density estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which replaces the traditional Gaussian function used in kernel density estimation with the Von Mises-Fisher distribution. We repeat the same procedure for both <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a relative angle determing the transition between two poses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All spherical kernel density estimations are performed on a set of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10,000</annotation></semantics></math> rotations using a fixed bandwidth <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0.08</annotation></semantics></math>. The log-probability density estimates computed at the vertices of each sphere are subject to a power normalization with exponent <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. For ease of visualization, we set <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=60</annotation></semantics></math> for the poses of AMASS, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=50</annotation></semantics></math> for NRMF, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=20</annotation></semantics></math> for NRDF, HuMoR, and all the transition densities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Poses and transitions are randomly selected from AMASS, and generated with all the other methods. For NRMF, we select <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10,000</annotation></semantics></math> samples from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>195</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">195</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math>-frames-long motion sequences generated starting from initial poses of AMASS motions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the evaluation of the pose <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> and transition <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\dot{\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\dot{\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> distributions, we also explicitly visualize a selected subsets of motion sequences and their full states <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We choose the most common pose in AMASS, denoted by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, as the starting point for all trajectories and methods. We then perform a <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-nearest neighbor (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN) search using <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}^{N_{J}}(\bm{\theta}_{c},\bm{\theta}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}^{N_{J}}(\bm{\theta}_{c},\bm{\theta}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to geodesically measure distances and correctly identify the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math> closest poses to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. These poses can occur at any timestep of a motion sequence as long as there are enough timesteps to complete a <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> frames motion. To promote diversity among the motions to represent, we also perform a <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}^{N_{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>SO</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{SO}(3)}^{N_{J}}</annotation></semantics></math>-based farthest point sampling with respect to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and all the poses at <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+20</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from the motion sequences identified with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN. This allows us to identify the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps originating as close as possible to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and terminating the farthest apart from each other –and their origin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The trajectories are geodesically traced onto the spheres using FlipOut, the edge flipping algorithm introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> to find shortest geodesics on polyhedral surfaces. Note that we rely on a discrete method for geodesic computations because the spheres are effectively discretized into ico-spheres for visualization purposes. We break the trajectory in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> segments each containing the same number of timesteps. Therefore, longer segments equate to faster transitions. Accelerations are finally represented mapping the three components of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ddot{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ddot{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the RGB space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As it can be observed in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Test-time optimization ‣ 4 NRMF as Versatile Generative Priors ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, HuMoR tends to overestimate the range of motion of each joint, often enabling poses which are never or rarely represented in the training data. NRDF significantly mitigates this issue, closely mimicking the behavior of AMASS on most joints. Nevertheless, NRDF appears the best at capturing the true pose distribution. A similar trend is observable for transitions, which not only appear slightly improved in terms of distributions in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Test-time optimization ‣ 4 NRMF as Versatile Generative Priors ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, but also in terms of individual examples reported in <a href="#S2.F9" title="In Pseudocode of RMF-Integrator ‣ B Algorithms, Architecture &amp; Details ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. In fact, the arrow orientations of NRMF more closely align with those of AMASS. Most notably, when comparing accelerations, we note that while NRMF can properly capture their variability, HuMoR seems to erroneously produce transitions with almost-fixed angular accelerations. These results prove once again the superiority of our method compared to the state of the art as well as the necessity of adopting a motion model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C </span>Additional Evaluations</h2>

<div id="S3a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we provide more qualitative results for our motion estimation. As shown in <a href="#S2.F11" title="In Training details ‣ B Algorithms, Architecture &amp; Details ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and <a href="#S2.F12" title="In Training details ‣ B Algorithms, Architecture &amp; Details ‣ Geometric Neural Distance Fields for Learning Human Motion Priors" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, we present additional qualitative comparisons on motion estimation and in-betweening. Our method consistently outperforms existing approaches such as HuMoR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, RoHM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and PhaseMP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, producing more realistic and physically plausible motions. In scenarios with partial or noisy observations, our predictions exhibit improved temporal consistency and anatomically accurate transitions, especially in challenging regions like occluded body parts. For motion in-betweening and motion generation, NRMF generates smooth and coherent transitions between sparse keyframes, capturing natural dynamics and avoiding artifacts or discontinuities often seen in baseline methods. These results further highlight the benefit of incorporating higher-order priors and respecting the underlying geometry of articulated motion.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1a" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motion In-betweening</h4>

<figure id="S3.T8" class="ltx_table">
